Chapter: Hpet
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The High Precision Event Timer, read as a clock rather than driven as a
 timer. This is a part we do not own, so the register offsets, the units
 and the enable bit are written down here; what this chapter decides for
 itself is left to the code.

 The base address is the conventional one and every machine this runs on
 places it there. The counter is 64 bits presented as two 32-bit halves
 at F0 and F4, and it counts at a rate the machine chooses and declares:
 GCAP_ID's upper half at offset 04 is the period of one tick in
 FEMTOSECONDS. That number is 69841279 under codex-vm and it is not the
 same on other silicon, which is why it is read rather than assumed.

 THE COUNTER DOES NOT RUN UNTIL BIT 0 OF THE CONFIGURATION IS SET, and
 firmware does not reliably leave it set. A reader that skips the enable
 gets a frozen counter, which is a plausible-looking constant rather than
 an error.

 THE 32-BIT HALF WRAPS EVERY FIVE MINUTES at codex-vm's rate, so the low
 half alone cannot time anything longer. It is read between two reads of
 the high half and taken again if the high half moved, which is the
 standard fix for reading a 64-bit counter through two 32-bit ports.

 We say:

Section: Registers

  hpet-base : Integer = #FED00000
  hpet-off-caps-period : Integer = #04
  hpet-off-config : Integer = #10
  hpet-off-counter-lo : Integer = #F0
  hpet-off-counter-hi : Integer = #F4

  hpet-config-enable : Integer = 1

  hpet-reg : Integer -> Integer
  hpet-reg (off) = bit-and (peek-32 (hpet-base + off) 0) #FFFFFFFF

Section: Starting

 Enabling an already-enabled timer is not a reset: the configuration is
 read, the bit is set in what was there, and a counter that was already
 running keeps its value.

  hpet-start : Integer
  hpet-start =
    let cfg = hpet-reg hpet-off-config
    in if bit-and cfg hpet-config-enable /= 0 then cfg
    else poke-32 (hpet-base + hpet-off-config) 0 (bit-or cfg hpet-config-enable)

Section: Reading

  hpet-ticks : Integer
  hpet-ticks =
    let hi1 = hpet-reg hpet-off-counter-hi
    in let lo = hpet-reg hpet-off-counter-lo
    in let hi2 = hpet-reg hpet-off-counter-hi
    in if hi1 == hi2 then hi1 * 4294967296 + lo
    else hi2 * 4294967296 + hpet-reg hpet-off-counter-lo

 A femtosecond is 10^-15 of a second, so the tick rate is that constant
 divided by the declared period. A machine that declares a zero period
 has told us nothing, and answering a huge number would be worse than
 answering none: the rate is reported as zero and the second reader
 below turns that into a clock that never advances rather than one that
 races.

  hpet-femtoseconds-per-second : Integer = 1000000000000000

  hpet-ticks-per-second : Integer
  hpet-ticks-per-second =
    let period = hpet-reg hpet-off-caps-period
    in if period <= 0 then 0
    else hpet-femtoseconds-per-second / period

  hpet-seconds : Integer
  hpet-seconds =
    let started = hpet-start
    in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
    in if rate <= 0 then 0
    else hpet-ticks / rate

